\f1\b\fs24 \cf2 Notes:
\f0\b0 \cf0 \
\
\'a5 The expression "Bring Out Yer Dead" is from a classic routine in the film "Monty Python & the Holy Grail" (which is itself based on a description in Daniel DeFoe's "Journal of the Plague Year" for that matter). I hope it does not offend.\
\
\'a5 Make sure you have had Spotlight index your computer!\
\
\'a5 The very fine {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.kainjow.com/blog/blosxom.cgi/2005/05/04#spodlight"}}{\fldrslt 
\f1\b Spodlight}} by Kevin Wojniak is an application that will allow Spotlight to index your iPod. Bring Out Yer Dead is able to copy files from your iPod when indexed this way.\
\
\'a5 Only one track can be selected at a time. I had thought of allowing several tracks to be selected, but the search results could get messy. Perhaps later.\
\
\'a5 Artwork is not preserved by the script. However, if a new file contains artwork, well, there it is.\
\
\'a5 I ran into a problem escaping single-quotes and double-quotes in tag names with "quoted form of" for the search routine, so the workaround was to have the script escape these beforehand. I don't know if this is a bug with "quoted form of" in Tiger or if I am Just Going About It The Wrong Way.\
\
\'a5 The script will detect if your selected track is a "dead track". It would not be prudent to attempt to re-add a file that already exists in your Music folder since a duplicate file may be created.\
\
\
